From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(crafting): expose Bedrock recipes and place ingredients

Display representable crafting-table recipes from Bedrock data with stable Java IDs and honor unlock events. Place ingredients through server-authoritative item stack requests, using tracked network IDs and recipe counts. Show a ghost recipe when ingredients are unavailable.

Manual crafting now resolves overlapping shapeless ingredients and recipe priority. Evaluate literal any_tag and all_tags Molang item queries against generated Bedrock item tags; leave unsupported expressions hidden. The server remains authoritative for the final craft.

Treat wildcard ingredient metadata as a wildcard when building Java recipe displays. Interpreting it as literal item metadata hid otherwise valid recipes.

Use the same placement logic for the player 2x2 grid so recipe book clicks work in both the player inventory and crafting table.

Batch shift crafting into one compatible inventory stack per request. Cap the recipe count by ingredient availability, stack capacity, and Bedrock action limits, then predict the same quantity locally until the server responds.

Include CraftResults immediately after CraftRecipe for both crafting-table
and player-grid requests. Geyser's InventoryTranslator requires this
transition before ingredient consumption and rejects a craft without it.
Keep each recipe result's per-craft amount separate from the batch count.

Verified against Geyser 2.11.3 source at
https://github.com/GeyserMC/Geyser/blob/63a4e2b79b12f0d138777d5fd80176a112b4bd72/core/src/main/java/org/geysermc/geyser/translator/inventory/InventoryTranslator.java
and focused request-order tests.

Keep transmitted block item metadata until connection-aware Item and
BlockLegacy resolution. CubeCraft Pillars of Fortune sends stripped oak
wood (runtime item ID -819) and its plank ingredient with auxiliary value
8. Native recipe comparison resolves block identities: auxiliary 8 and
canonical auxiliary 0 select the same default block. An executable check
of the actual stripped-oak-wood constructor, pillar-axis registration and
palette creation verifies canonical metadata 0/1/2 for y/x/z. The regression
now executes the production matcher after both item codecs, accepts both
0 and 8 for that default block, and rejects a different runtime axis.
It preserves server-assigned recipe IDs and the request path.

Expose protocol 2193 single-input furnace, blast-furnace and smoker recipes
in their own Java recipe-book categories, with station-specific stable
display IDs. Route recipe selection to ordinary Place actions on the
correct ingredient provider. Cooking and fuel/output updates remain server
owned. Combine compatible inventory stacks, preserve input variants, clear
mismatched input into existing stacks where possible, and plan atomically
before recording both inventories for acknowledgment or rejection recovery.
Use the negative request ID for a slot changed earlier in the same request,
including a source that becomes empty. Canonicalize hotbar, inventory and
combined-player references to the same physical slot for this tracking.
Reject recipes belonging to a different station.

Evidence: the pinned 1.26.51.1 protocol 2193 recipe catalog advertises cooking
recipes as tagged ShapelessRecipe entries. The matching 1.26.51-beta.1
protocol metadata documents client request IDs in ItemStackNetIdVariant.
Java 26.3 FurnaceRecipeDisplay.STREAM_CODEC has input, fuel, result, station,
VarInt duration and float experience, and recipe display registry ID 2.
CraftingData carries neither time nor XP, so those Java display-only fields
stay neutral. They do not replace Bedrock ContainerSetData, native fuel
rules, or cooking simulation. The add-on's native cooking screens and
recipe controls still need implementation and native/route verification.

Geyser InventoryTranslator at dd10af11b5d8f8bcc396dde0f2751534aa3f4ad7
checks ordinary transfer identities against server inventory and accepts
negative request references. Targeted tests cover typed/direct recipe
encoding, category/unlock routing, partial stack aggregation, capacity,
variant selection, stale identities, provider mappings and rejected requests.

Retain protocol 2193 furnace options per family and register packet 351 in
both directions. Keep tab selection and opaque enum values in native
state, and project only layout/filter fields into Java recipe-book
settings. Preserve other books when the server updates one family.
Evidence: 1.26.51-beta.1 protocol 2193 FurnaceOptions metadata and
matching gophertunnel 80c811b6186016b3860c358368cfa47e507f26e9 agree on
byte family, signed compressed tab, boolean filter and signed compressed
layout. Java 26.3 RecipeBookSettings orders crafting, furnace, blast
furnace and smoker boolean pairs. This projection is a compatibility
path; native screen controls and route comparisons remain incomplete.

Transport the retained native furnace options through a separately
negotiated protocol-2193 payload in both directions. Replay all retained
families after registration or entering play, echo Java/server changes,
and advertise the return channel for Fabric clients on direct and ViaProxy
connections. Withdraw the capability after client unregistration. Preserve
unknown native enum values without forcing them into Java book state.
Decode bounded native envelopes before dispatch so malformed input cannot
mutate preferences or become a server edit. Native furnace scene controls
and native/direct/ViaProxy behavior comparisons remain incomplete.

Allow ordinary crafting-result pickup onto a compatible cursor stack in
both player and table grids. Preserve the cursor's confirmed stack ID and
existing metadata, and require space for the full per-craft result before
consuming input. A normal click still makes one craft; Shift-click batches
available ingredients. Rejected requests restore both the cursor and grid.

The pinned Geyser 2.11.3 InventoryTranslator at
63a4e2b79b12f0d138777d5fd80176a112b4bd72 maps a CreatedOutput-to-cursor
Place action to one ordinary result-slot click, including an occupied
cursor. Parameterized handler tests cover both grids, network identities,
rollback, incompatible or full cursors, and two logs yielding eight planks
through two normal clicks or one Shift-click.

Apply target 1.26.51.1 recipe registration before presentation filtering.
Keep the first station/name pair and reject zero or reused network IDs
across stations. Unknown Multi UUIDs do not construct a recipe or reserve
an ID. Retain raw shaped dimensions and flat ingredients, then apply Java
grid limits only at matching, placement and display boundaries.

Bind presented books to their original catalog and unlock revision. Give
replacement registrations new click IDs so an older Java PLACE_RECIPE
cannot target a different payload after a clear/reload. Keep IDs stable
for the same surviving registration, never recycle removed IDs, and copy
ingredients and nested result data so callers cannot mutate that authority.

Decode recipe result counts through the native signed-short conversion
before storing their positive low byte. Counts 128 through 255 remain
nonempty. Validate every result against the current native item ID table
before claiming registration keys, including air, unknown IDs and empty
counts. Keep ordinary inventory decoding separate from recipe results.

Fresh probes execute the unchanged target duplicate guard, network-ID
guard, shaped dimension guard, result validity/count paths and Multi
factory dispatch. They cover 49 name cases, 72 network-mode cases, 217
dimension cases, 43 output-state cases, all 65,536 wire counts, all 256
stack counts and 142 Multi UUIDs. Initialized registry state, vector
boundaries, allocation boundaries and comparison seams remain supplied;
full factory construction, item ownership and native visual parity are
not established by these probes. The private fixture records those limits.

Use the connection's native Item construction context for recipe results
as well as inventory stacks. The result codec remains clientbound-only.
Verify the complete signed-short count sweep through the native registry
constructor, rather than a numeric-only stand-in. The stripped-wood grid
regression constructs both wire auxiliary values 0 and 8 into canonical
stack metadata 0 before comparing the default Block and another axis.

Collect cooking sources by the constructed Item's concrete descriptor hash.
Cache the first nonempty stack's eligibility for later matching descriptors,
including rejected groups. Exclude an initial enchantment ListTag or nonzero
ByteTag item lock, then sort groups by descending available count with the
native tie ordering. Retain source container and slot identities.

Use this collector in furnace, smoker and blast-furnace recipe placement.
Keep full stack compatibility and authoritative request/rollback checks at
the transfer boundary; a descriptor group does not authorize merging NBT.

The unchanged pinned 1.26.51.1 cooking collector verifies 3,789 private
cases, including 1,176 cases through its actual typed-byte lookup over
supplied compound trees. The fixture supplies ownership, container access,
generic NBT presence, allocation/CRT/destruction and result-name seams.
It excludes native compound construction, block-derived collection cases,
full transaction/controller execution and visible native parity. Authored
cooking catalog and screen activation remain follow-up work.

Evaluate native cooking source presence before selected-recipe placement.
Keep station input, furnace fuel, hotbar and inventory providers in native
order, with cursor admission controlled separately. A usable alternative
returns state 0/1 under filtering/no filtering; missing or invalid output
returns 3/2. Presence does not establish transfer capacity or recipe count.
Reject unresolved output ownership and the actual native minecraft:air name
before querying providers or starting an inventory request.

The unchanged target evaluator, empty/air guard and complete collector
execute 6,072 private cases. Java compares all 4,272 legal bool-flag cases;
1,800 non-boolean byte controls remain research only. Recipe alternative
extraction, provider/map access, ownership and runtime seams are supplied.
The native name initializer independently confirms the air guard's full
identifier. Full output-alternative extraction, authored cooking catalog,
controller activation and visible native parity remain follow-up work.

Expand cooking inputs through native descriptor visitation semantics.
Named descriptors resolve with their declared auxiliary value first, then
construct count-one Items with auxiliary zero and their default association.
Use the constructed concrete queries for both availability and placement;
matching the original declaration selected the wrong inventory variant.
Keep normal crafting's named-ingredient semantics unchanged.

The complete target extractor146989830, output matcher14248dac0, native
named resolution and ItemInstance constructor14247ead0 execute 252 cases:
108 ordinary/concrete/named expansions, 48 associated-default cases, and
96 output-selection controls. Java compares all 96 named/unknown expansion
cases, including null auxiliary admission and reassigned block ownership.
Controlled recipe trees, world/result accessors, captured Item registry
storage, warm associated caches and runtime services are supplied. Full
factory installation, tag/Molang visitation order, nonempty output data,
complete output-alternative catalog and authored UI remain unverified.
Tag and Molang membership retain the existing predicates; only the new
constructed input query replaces raw declared metadata during cooking.

Build a station-local catalog from the current registration and unlock
revision. Match each displayed network output against every unlocked
recipe at that station, then concatenate constructed input queries in
unsigned UTF-8 recipe order. Reject stale snapshots and invalid outputs.
Keep Java placement attached to its selected registered recipe until
native quantity and cursor actions are implemented.

Output matching uses query-sided Block identity, wildcard auxiliary,
restriction identities, shield timing and native NBT equality. Root
quantity and network IDs do not select an output. Signed floating zeroes
compare equal, NaNs compare unequal even through the same compound, and
empty list element types remain significant. Full retained network data
determines charged-item loading within the stable connection registry;
independently mutated native child stacks are outside this comparison.

Original target 1.26.51.1 routines verify 361 output comparisons after
userdata installation, including 217 rows with charged children, and 96
whole-extractor output controls. Java also compares 3278 valid-UTF-8 NBT
cases against original virtual comparators. The fixtures supply captured
registry storage, generic Item definitions, NBT trees and runtime services.
Malformed native UTF-8, pointer-hash collisions, full factory installation,
authored screen activation and native action parity remain unverified.

Publish authoritative cooking views only after the exact opened menu
receives its first server contents. Reuse one station catalog per update,
retain complete translated recipe previews, and preserve native category,
preference and progress models independently of Java furnace properties.
Negotiate both cooking channels before emitting snapshots. Ordinary
clients retain their existing screens and packet path.

Bind publications to the inventory owner, container object and lifetime,
recipe registry/unlock revision, server item revisions and deep item
snapshots, resource program, and preferences. Deep snapshots also detect
in-place predictions. Reject authority changes during item conversion;
suppress recursive publication and retire state on close or withdrawal.
Flush real Java content before its derived cooking view. Progress-only
updates retain item authority and use a separate small revisioned message.

This supplies the production model and transport. Client authored cooking
screens, activation, search/navigation controls, and native quantity/cursor
actions remain unfinished and are not inferred from Java screen controls.

Return mismatched cooking input with the shared source-first occupied-slot
merge predicate. Raw stack equality rejected auxiliary variants of items
that do not stack by data, and accepted damaged occupied destinations that
native stacking rejects. Keep recipe-query matching and Java recipe-book
placement quantities separate from this ordinary return transfer.

The pinned 1.26.51.1 transfer engine at 1433e8380 uses occupied-stack
predicate 142488fe0 with the moving source first. Reuse ItemRewriter's
constructed stacking properties and canonical auxiliary resolution. Two
regressions fail before this change: native auxiliary merging with a full
inventory, including request references and rejection recovery, and atomic
refusal when only a damaged occupied destination has capacity.

Assign request references after every predicted slot mutation. The pinned
1.26.51.1 executable uses identity callback147c9ac00 and observer147c9aa20
from the client manager's predicted containers. Those callbacks assign
the negative request ID to both endpoints, even an emptied source. Later
transfers in the same request use that reference instead of an earlier
positive stack ID or zero. Track physical player slots across provider
aliases; cooking sources use hotbar28/inventory29 and returns use combined12.

The earlier base-manager serialization fixture omitted those callbacks.
Its preserved positive IDs describe unpredicted backing and cannot justify
production prediction semantics. Replace that comparison with five fresh
payload controls using native predicted-container construction1425ac0b0,
ClientScreenData construction1425bdf10, original callbacks and mutation,
payload conversion14273fc70, context construction14273ad30 and packet
writer14273aab0/14273a2d0. Java matches the complete cooking payload.

The manager uses flags1,1 and the native client vtable. Base construction
runs before Level attachment and takes its ordinary null-Level branch.
Subclass fields, one-entity registry, screen bindings, packet view and
BinaryStream fields are controlled data from native layouts. Full Player
and screen construction, negotiated flag provenance, predicted request
finalization, the enclosing sender, transport and live acceptance remain
unverified by those open-request controls. This does not complete native
authored cooking actions.

A stronger fresh fixture uses the request manager's original owned registry,
original entity creation1425c0460 and weak-reference construction1425c06e0.
Native finish1425b0cf0 and detach1425b0e50 release exactly one retained weak
reference and queue the unchanged Place actions. Enabled flush1425b1f70
converts the request, constructs the actual packet, clears and destroys
the queue before transport, and cleans up the converted payload afterward.
An external transport sink calls the original serializer on that packet.
An empty follow-up flush emits nothing. The Java planner/codec comparison
now reads this finalized native cooking payload byte for byte through
STACKANVIL_NATIVE_FINALIZED_REQUEST_WIRE.

Typed-storage and client-subclass initialization still follow controlled
inline layouts. Screen container bindings, transport and BinaryStream
fields remain supplied. Full Player/screen construction, component signals,
negotiated mode provenance, outer input/frame scope, real transport and
server acceptance remain unverified. Preserve those limits separately
from the now-executed finish, flush and ownership-release functions.

Execute snapshot-bound native primary cooking actions through their own
selection planner and the ordinary transfer batch API. Compare the clicked
output with the retained selection before the ingredient-only branch.
Toggle an existing selection by returning ingredient and output items;
otherwise choose unlocked alternatives through native descriptor groups,
collect from fuel/hotbar/inventory, and return output after placement.
Each return replaces the player history projection. The final output
return therefore captures post-placement history, while selection-only
admission leaves the constructor's initially empty history unchanged.

Emit one owned request per transfer with physical provider indices and
negative references for every previously changed slot, including empty
slots and combined-player aliases. Retain intermediate predictions for
responses and restore physical items and selection on failed submission.
Keep the Java recipe-book increment/max behavior independent.

Consume version-4 cooking actions only on negotiated authoritative
connections. Require the exact presentation, current items, unlocked
recipe inputs and authored cell command. Deduplicate request sequences;
follow-up phases retire the retained gesture without repeating transfer
effects. Defer intermediate publication and bind highlights to the current
visible recipe ordering. Menu generations prevent a late release from
changing a replacement menu.

Evidence: Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372/protocol 2193. Native entry
146c9a3c0 compares the selected output before 146c46900, and 14c54c950
returns both physical collections before clearing it. Original manager
construction initializes its history vector empty. A fresh enabled native
prediction control executes six press/release steps, automatic scopes,
owned finalization, and actual flush/packet serialization between steps.
It verifies selection toggle, combined-provider return, post-placement
history and refill request references. The Java primary action matches
the original two-source native packet byte for byte.

The Fabric cooking presentation remains passive. Physical device bindings,
nonempty-cursor branches, cross-input prediction, authoritative model
watchers, full controller/frame ownership and live route acceptance remain
unfinished. This is a production backend milestone, not complete native
cooking or five-area parity.

Use recipe descriptor matching for source admission. Keep full Item
queries for selected-output comparison, ingredient selection, history,
and the transfer loop's stop at a different item variant. Capture history
through the empty-ingredient return before filling as the native code
does, so an output can restore an emptied source slot with its request
reference instead of choosing a new hotbar destination.

Negotiate cooking actions in the correct payload direction. Client model,
progress and result receivers enable a separately advertised serverbound
action channel. Do not require a client receiver for its outgoing action.
Retain play-state and server-authoritative inventory guards, suppress
repeated advertisements, and retire held input when a receiver withdraws.
Re-registration cannot release a gesture from the withdrawn capability.
Loss of inventory authority also withdraws the capability and presentation.
Packet regressions exercise real registration and action handlers. The
original direction check rejects the valid request without transferring.
Fabric cooking controls and live action acceptance remain incomplete.

Publish cooking captions through the shared native tab-label rules. The
search caption depends on both station family and the current filtering
flag; the inventory tab uses the native shared survival key. Preserve
item and opening authority while preference changes advance the model.

The publisher regression covers all three station families, both search
and inventory tabs, and both filter states across complete program
round trips. The old publisher fails and the shared-rule publisher passes.
Native getter parity is bound by sixty unchanged machine-code controls
in the shared UI prerequisite, with explicit allocator and CRT seams.

Retain the native mode-two recipe focus before a primary selection. That
focus permits the following authored selection with a carried Item while
direct presses in the other verified modes keep their empty-cursor guard.
Bind terminal focus release to the exact menu, presentation and input
context. Capability loss or menu replacement retires the permission.
Focus does not transfer or manufacture a new selection snapshot.

Pass focused admission explicitly into the existing native placement and
return planner. Preserve the carried Item in every request prediction and
retain existing server-authority, source, network-ID and revision checks.
Five fresh 1.26.51.1 cases execute the original full graph, held SlotInfo
getter, focus entry, selection effects, provider transfers, owned request
scopes and native flush/serialization. Mode-two carried counts 0/1/64 use
two Place requests for seven hotbar and five inventory ingredients without
changing the cursor; carried controls in modes one and three do nothing.

Native typed model/controller fields, recipe/result caches and world
interfaces remain supplied, along with abstract client mode/no-player
interfaces and prior startup/CRT/transport boundaries. No state predicate,
focus/selection effect, transfer or serialization function is replaced.
Complete controller construction, physical input mapping, server
acknowledgments and native pixels remain separate verification work.

Recheck the complete player-UI snapshot and its server revision after
planning. Item metadata callbacks can otherwise replace the carried Item
between focus admission and committing its requests. Reject that stale
plan without mutating station/player items or restoring over the newer
cursor; a fresh retry retains the original selection history.
